    "en": {
      "category": "Health and prevention",
      "title": "If you're bleeding, snap a photo on your phone first",
      "excerpt": "Blood in your stool, blood in what you threw up — there's one easy thing people almost always forget to do, and it genuinely helps us a lot.",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "\"I think there was blood in my stool.\" \"What I threw up looked kind of red...\" I hear this often in clinic, but honestly, **the memory of \"how red it was\" or \"how much there was\" usually isn't very reliable.** Human memory tends to get hazy exactly when we're most alarmed."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "By the end of this post, you'll know one small thing that makes a real difference when you notice blood — in your stool or in vomit — and need to see a doctor. The short version: **before you flush or rinse it away, take a quick photo on your phone.** That alone can completely change how accurately we can assess things."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "\\* This advice is for situations mild or moderate enough that you can calmly make your way in. **If something feels seriously wrong — you're feeling faint, the bleeding won't stop, you're breaking out in a cold sweat — call emergency services (119) before worrying about a photo.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### \"It was red\" alone doesn't actually tell us much"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Being startled by what you just saw and going blank — that's a completely natural reaction. But honestly, with only a memory of \"it was pretty red\" or \"there seemed to be a lot,\" it's genuinely hard for a doctor to judge how seriously to take it. Was it bright red or darker, almost black? Just a faint streak, or enough to discolor the whole bowl? That visual detail is exactly what helps narrow down the cause."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### In defense of watermelon and red wine"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Between us — it's genuinely common for someone to rush in worried about blood in their stool, only to find it was watermelon seeds or red pigment from food eaten the day before. The reverse happens too: iron supplements or bismuth-containing medications can turn stool black, and that color change can actually be a sign of real bleeding. A single photo can clear up both the false alarms and the real ones, right there on the spot. Watermelon and red wine are entirely innocent."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### There's really no need to feel embarrassed"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "\"Is it even okay to take a photo of this?\" \"It's embarrassing to show someone.\" I completely understand that feeling. But stool and vomit are things we, as medical staff, see as a routine part of the job. Accurate information is worth far more than any embarrassment. Please don't hesitate — snap the photo and bring it in."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### If you coughed up blood, that's a bit different"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Everything above was mainly about blood in your stool or vomit. **Coughing up blood (hemoptysis) can be a sign of an airway problem even in a small amount, and calls for different caution than blood from the digestive tract.** If you have any shortness of breath along with it, please get in touch or come in right away, rather than taking time to find your phone first."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### A message from the director"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Of course, if you're feeling faint, the bleeding is heavy and ongoing, or you're breaking out in a cold sweat — **anything that feels seriously wrong — please call emergency services (119) right away, before worrying about whether you have time for a photo.** A photo is a helpful ally for situations where you can calmly make your way in to be seen, not a reason to delay care."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Next time you notice something and think, \"wait, is that blood?\" — take a breath, and snap one photo. That small action can genuinely help your care."
        }
      ]
    }
